# Desktop Entry Specification File
# Purpose: Integrates the OpenCode Script Extractor into Linux desktop application launcher menus (GNOME, KDE, XFCE, Cinnamon).
# Format: FreeDesktop.org Desktop Entry Specification standard key-value file format.
#
# Testing and installation commands for system integration:
#   - Validate syntax: `desktop-file-validate opencode-script-extractor.desktop`
#   - Install user shortcut: `cp opencode-script-extractor.desktop ~/.local/share/applications/`
#   - Update desktop database: `update-desktop-database ~/.local/share/applications/`
#   - Launch via desktop launcher service: `gtk-launch opencode-script-extractor.desktop`
#   - Install system-wide: `sudo desktop-file-install --dir=/usr/share/applications opencode-script-extractor.desktop`
#
# Optional FreeDesktop keys that can be added (not currently active):
#   - `Keywords=opencode;script;extractor;sqlite;export;` : Search keywords for launcher search.
#   - `StartupWMClass=OpenCode Script Extractor` : Window manager class name to map running window to dock icon.
#   - `NoDisplay=true` : Keep application installed but hide from application launcher menus.
#   - `Hidden=true` : Completely mark application as deleted/disabled in desktop environment.
#   - `MimeType=application/x-sqlite3;text/plain;` : File types associated with this application for right-click opening.
#   - `Version=1.5` : Desktop Entry Specification specification version marker.

# Line 32 explanation: `[Desktop Entry]`
# - What it does: Defines the main header section group for the desktop launcher configuration.
# - Options: `[Desktop Entry]` is mandatory. Additional group sections can exist like `[Desktop Action ActionName]` for right-click context menu shortcuts.
# - Default value: `[Desktop Entry]`
# - Output / Side effect: Identifies file format to Linux desktop menu parsers.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If missing or misspelled, desktop managers fail to parse the desktop file.
# - How to test: Validate with `desktop-file-validate opencode-script-extractor.desktop`.
[Desktop Entry]

# Line 35 explanation: `Type=Application`
# - What it does: Specifies the fundamental item type of this desktop shortcut.
# - Options:
#   * `Type=Application` (Current default): Launches an executable binary or shell script.
#   * `Type=Link`: Opens a web URL link in the default internet browser.
#   * `Type=Directory`: Defines folder icon metadata for file managers.
# - Default value: `Application`
# - Output / Side effect: Instructs desktop environment to treat entry as runnable software.
# - Edge cases & Errors: Invalid type value causes desktop validation error and hides shortcut from application launcher.
# - How to test: Run `desktop-file-validate opencode-script-extractor.desktop`.
Type=Application

# Line 40 explanation: `Name=OpenCode Script Extractor`
# - What it does: The display name text string shown under the desktop icon, in taskbars, and in launcher search menus.
# - Options: Any text string (e.g. `Name=OpenCode Extractor`). Localized variants like `Name[fr]=Extracteur OpenCode` or `Name[es]=Extractor OpenCode` can be added beneath it.
# - Default value: `OpenCode Script Extractor`
# - Output / Side effect: Renders application title in system desktop UI.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If empty or missing, desktop environment falls back to displaying filename `opencode-script-extractor.desktop`.
# - How to test: Search for "OpenCode Script Extractor" in GNOME overview or KDE app launcher.
Name=OpenCode Script Extractor

# Line 44 explanation: `Comment=Extract scripts and code files from OpenCode session history and subagents`
# - What it does: Explanatory tooltip text displayed when hovering the mouse cursor over the application icon in launcher menus.
# - Options: Any freeform descriptive text line. Localized variants like `Comment[fr]=...` or `Comment[de]=...` are also supported.
# - Default value: `Extract scripts and code files from OpenCode session history and subagents`
# - Output / Side effect: Displays tooltip box on hover.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If empty or omitted, no tooltip is shown on hover.
# - How to test: Hover cursor over icon in GNOME Applications view or system main menu.
Comment=Extract scripts and code files from OpenCode session history and subagents

# Line 52 explanation: `Exec=/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR/opencode-script-extractor.sh`
# - What it does: Defines the exact absolute file path command executed when the user launches this desktop shortcut.
# - Options & Field Code variants:
#   * Absolute path: `/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR/opencode-script-extractor.sh`
#   * With field code `%f`: `Exec=/home/.../opencode-script-extractor.sh %f` (passes a single dropped file path).
#   * With field code `%F`: `Exec=/home/.../opencode-script-extractor.sh %F` (passes multiple dropped file paths).
#   * With field code `%u`: `Exec=/home/.../opencode-script-extractor.sh %u` (passes a single URL).
#   * With field code `%U`: `Exec=/home/.../opencode-script-extractor.sh %U` (passes multiple URLs).
#   * With Qt flags: `Exec=/home/.../opencode-script-extractor.sh -platform offscreen`
# - Default value: `/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR/opencode-script-extractor.sh`
# - Output / Side effect: Launches the bash script which in turn executes Python PyQt6 GUI main window (`gui/__main__.py`).
# - Edge cases & Errors: If path does not exist or lacks execute permissions (`chmod +x`), OS shows "Failed to launch application" dialog or error in system log.
# - How to test: Run `gtk-launch opencode-script-extractor.desktop` in terminal to simulate desktop launch.
Exec=/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR/opencode-script-extractor.sh

# Line 59 explanation: `Path=/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR`
# - What it does: Specifies the initial working directory folder where the launched process will execute.
# - Options: Any valid absolute folder directory path (e.g. `/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR`).
# - Default value: `/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR`
# - Output / Side effect: Sets initial working directory context for the launcher shell process.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If folder directory path does not exist, system defaults working directory to user home directory (`~`).
# - How to test: Test script relative file access when starting application from menu.
Path=/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR

# Line 65 explanation: `Icon=utilities-terminal`
# - What it does: The visual icon image graphic shown next to the application name in launcher menus and dock taskbars.
# - Options:
#   * Theme icon name: `utilities-terminal` (Current default), `system-file-manager`, `python3`, `text-editor`, `utilities-system-monitor`.
#   * Absolute path to PNG/SVG file: `Icon=/home/ficus-pro/Documents/OC-SCRIPT-EXTRACTOR/gui/assets/icon.png`.
# - Default value: `utilities-terminal`
# - Output / Side effect: Renders terminal icon graphic in desktop menu panels and window titles.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If icon name or path cannot be resolved, system falls back to a generic default application gear icon.
# - How to test: Inspect icon graphic rendered in GNOME shell dock or KDE taskbar.
Icon=utilities-terminal

# Line 70 explanation: `Terminal=false`
# - What it does: Determines whether a visible command line terminal window (like xterm or gnome-terminal) opens to display stdout/stderr log output while the application runs.
# - Options:
#   * `Terminal=false` (Current default): Runs GUI application silently without opening a terminal window.
#   * `Terminal=true`: Opens terminal window displaying Python print() outputs and log tracebacks (useful during development/debugging).
# - Default value: `false`
# - Output / Side effect: Suppresses auxiliary terminal window creation.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If `Terminal=true` is set on a headless server without a terminal emulator installed, launch may fail.
# - How to test: Temporarily set `Terminal=true`, double click shortcut, and verify terminal window pops up alongside GUI.
Terminal=false

# Line 76 explanation: `Categories=Development;`
# - What it does: Assigns classification category tags used by OS desktop menus to organize applications into submenus (e.g. Development, Utilities).
# - Options: Standard categories separated by semicolons:
#   * `Categories=Development;` (Current default)
#   * `Categories=Development;Utility;`
#   * `Categories=System;IDE;`
# - Default value: `Development;`
# - Output / Side effect: Places application under "Programming" or "Development" submenu in Linux desktop application menus.
# - Edge cases & Errors: Unknown category tags are ignored or placed into "Other" menu group.
# - How to test: Check menu group placement in KDE Application Launcher or XFCE Whisker Menu.
Categories=Development;

# Line 81 explanation: `StartupNotify=true`
# - What it does: Enables visual desktop startup notification (shows spinning cursor or taskbar animation while the application process initializes).
# - Options:
#   * `StartupNotify=true` (Current default): Shows busy loading cursor until main GUI window displays.
#   * `StartupNotify=false`: Launches process silently without mouse cursor feedback.
# - Default value: `true`
# - Output / Side effect: Displays loading mouse cursor feedback during launch startup sequence.
# - Edge cases & Errors: If application fails to signal startup completion, watch cursor may spin for 10 seconds before timing out.
# - How to test: Observe cursor state immediately after clicking application launcher entry.
StartupNotify=true
